  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p>At 6,079 feet, <strong>Craggy Dome</strong> is a summit in North Carolina. The Craggy Dome Trail covers 0.7 miles (loop) with about 250 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a steady climb. It's rated Easy overall.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>The climbing is spread fairly evenly from start to finish rather than stacked into one steep section. The steepest stretch arrives around mile 0.29, gaining roughly 1,500 feet per mile \u2014 expect a slow, deliberate pace through here. From the trailhead at about 5,738 feet, the route tops out near 5,850 feet. Along the way the route passes a viewpoint (Lower Overlook) near mile 0, a viewpoint (Craggy Dome Scenic Overlook (MP 364.5)) near mile 0, a viewpoint (Craggy Dome Overlook (MP 364.1)) near mile 0.31, a viewpoint (Craggy Pinnacle Summit) near mile 0.68 \u2014 useful checkpoints for judging your pace.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=35.70272,-82.37781\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">35.70272, -82.37781</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 10 miles northeast of Asheville, North Carolina. Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Easy rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 0.5 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Hallback, Mount Mitchell and Mount Craig \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
